      "Language": "ger",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "Reliable reduction of pain, observance of anatomical variations, avoidance of complications."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INDICATIONS",
          "content": "Failure of conservative treatment, clinically obvious diagnosis, positive Finkelstein test (pathognomonic)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "CONTRAINDICATIONS",
          "content": "Previous splitting of the retinaculum of the first dorsal compartment, uncertainty of diagnosis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SURGICAL TECHNIQUE",
          "content": "Skin incision with protection of the branches of the superficial radial nerve, exposure of the first dorsal compartment, w-shaped incision and preparation of the retinaculum lobes, exposure of all tendons, inspection of the whole compartment and possible anatomic variations. Loose adaptation of the lobes, irrigation, hemostasis, skin closing."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "POSTOPERATIVE MANAGEMENT",
          "content": "Consequent immobilization in a thumb cast for a total of 3 weeks (+\u20093 weeks optional depending on load/profession). Sutures removed after 2 weeks, then the patient can start mobilization exercises (without the splint) after 3 weeks."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Between August 2005 and July 2007, 38 patients (40 wrists) were operated without complications. No tendon subluxation/dislocation, one wrist with persistent slightly positive Finkelstein test, in all other cases complete relief of the symptoms. All patients would elect to repeat the surgery."
        }
      ]},
